## CROSS-CUTTING — Category J: Performance & Architecture

**38. The Alerts page drawer makes 5+ reads per click — pre-fetch the common ones.**
Lines 405-462: opening a drawer fetches rules, delivery status, history, re-check SQL, and remediation context. Each is a separate `run()`. Batch the first 3 (rules + delivery + history) into one `run_batch` since they're independent.

*Reasoning:* A 2-second drawer open feels slow. Parallel batching could reduce it to <1 second. This is the most-interacted-with component on the page.

**39. Security page at 1018 lines should be split like Cost was.**
Similar to the Operations recommendation: 10 sections in one file is maintenance debt. Split into `security_parts/posture.py`, `security_parts/access.py`, `security_parts/exposure.py`, `security_parts/trust_center.py`.

*Reasoning:* 1018 lines with 10 sections means merge conflicts, testing difficulty, and cognitive overload when making changes.

**40. Both pages should share a "resolution workflow" component instead of implementing it separately.**
Alerts has ACK/RESOLVE/lifecycle. Incidents have OPEN/MITIGATED/RESOLVED. Security findings will eventually need lifecycle too. Create a shared `lifecycle_controls(entity_type, entity_id, current_status, allowed_transitions)` component.

*Reasoning:* Three separate lifecycle implementations means three places to fix bugs, three places to add audit logging, three places to update the UI. A shared component centralizes the pattern.

